{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2WtNbQZTF4ekYndVDJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Mc3c14zGZpStuPanPjVY78","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174801__xc8e150/work/PLAN.md","offset":797,"limit":240},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8108,"cache_read_input_tokens":430715,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8108},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619","uuid":"e1067fa5-ef31-4aa2-9a2b-34d6c02455ea","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:33:38.639Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2Wt8TQ2ZnVtsqvFxRD","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Mc3c14zGZpStuPanPjVY78":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174801__xc8e150/work/PLAN.md","offset":797,"limit":240}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mc3c14zGZpStuPanPjVY78","type":"tool_result","content":"797\t| `radio` | a story line (§4.18) |\n798\t| `tip` | a first-time tutorial tip (once per save, unless \"Show tips\" is on) |\n799\t| `flash` | the oncoming last train flashes its headlights; answer with the horn within 4 s for +2 s (S5) |\n800\t\n801\t**Tests:** each event fires once in its stage under the autopilot playthrough; crossing gates are down while our train is on the crossing; the obstruction\n802\tclears only after we stop; the horn makes workers wave; door rush adds 8 s to that dwell.\n803\t\n804\t### 4.12 Time of day, weather and light (`40-sound.js` for audio, `rail.js` for the image)\n805\t\n806\t**Time of day** is a float hour from the stage (or Free Run). It picks one of these grades, blended linearly between neighbours:\n807\t\n808\t| grade | hours | sky (top → horizon) | ambient | notes |\n809\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n810\t| dawn | 5–7 | #2a2850 → #f0a080 | .70 warm | low sun east, long shadows (darker ground bands) |\n811\t| day | 8–15 | #3a78d8 → #bfe0ff | 1.0 | |\n812\t| afternoon haze | 14–16 | #5a8ad0 → #e8e0c8 | .95 | |\n813\t| sunset | 17–18.5 | #3a2a6a → #ff9a4a | .75 orange | big low sun on the backdrop; glare zones |\n814\t| night | 19.5–4.5 | #060818 → #1a2440 | .30 blue | stars, moon, city glow; lit windows; headlights; platform lights stay bright |\n815\t\n816\t- The backdrop is an `E.Backdrop` with layers per zone (mountains, hills, city, sea, clouds). Its `turn` scrolls with the curve × distance travelled.\n817\t- **Rain** (S2, Free Run):\n818\t  - darker grade (−20%); streaks and windshield droplets that build up;\n819\t  - wipers sweep every 1.2 s in the cab;\n820\t  - wet rails; puddles on platforms (bright blots);\n821\t  - umbrellas on the crowds;\n822\t  - rain hiss in the audio, and lower adhesion (§4.3).\n823\t- **Fog:** distance fog, larger signal glows, short sighting. Hoshino: \"When you can't see, the signals see for you.\"\n824\t- **Glare:** an additive white-orange bloom over the upper half, scaled by `cos(heading − sunHeading)`. The sun visor animates down.\n825\t\n826\t**Tests:** the grade blend is continuous at every boundary hour; rain sets the adhesion; fog sets sighting to 180 m.\n827\t\n828\t### 4.13 Cameras and the director (`60-drive.js`, `61-attract.js`)\n829\t\n830\t| camera | placement | use |\n831\t|---|---|---|\n832\t| `cab` | s_front − 1.5, x = track, h 2.9, horizon .40 | gameplay (default) |\n833\t| `cabMin` | the same, without the dashboard | \"Minimal\" cab display setting; duel viewports |\n834\t| `chase` | behind the rear by 25 m, h 9, x +2, fov 70 | View button; attract |\n835\t| `chaseHigh` | rear − 40 m, h 22, x +6 | quad-track shots, the hub card |\n836\t| `platform` | on the platform 15 m past the stop target, h 1.7, `dir: -1` | View button at stations; attract; the S5 finale |\n837\t| `trackside` | 4 m beside the line at a scripted s ahead, h 2, `dir: -1`; holds while the train passes, then cuts | attract, ending |\n838\t| `stopCam` | 2D side view (§4.6) | after every stop |\n839\t\n840\t- `view` cycles through cab → chase → platform (platform only near a station) → cab.\n841\t- **Cab feel:**\n842\t  - lateral sway ∝ curve × v²;\n843\t  - 1-px vertical bumps at rail joints (every 25 m, timed with the joint sound);\n844\t  - the view pitches 2–3 px forward under braking and back under power (`pitchPx` follows the smoothed acceleration);\n845\t  - a jolt on EB.\n846\t  - The \"Camera sway\" setting scales these; reduce motion turns them off.\n847\t- **Look:** the right stick or a right-drag shifts `lookX` up to ±25% of W and `lookY` ±10% of H, springing back. It's a glance, not a free camera.\n848\t- **Director** (attract, ending): a list of shots `{ cam, dur, at }`, cut with 0.3 s dissolves.\n849\t\n850\t**Tests:** each camera projects our train's front to the expected screen side; `view` cycles only through the cameras that apply; the director runs\n851\tits shots in order.\n852\t\n853\t### 4.14 Cab, HUD, stop gauge (`50-cab.js`, `51-hud.js`)\n854\t\n855\t**10-foot rules:**\n856\t- Nothing smaller than the 5×7 font at scale 1. Key numbers (speed, stop distance, Halo Time, notch) at scale 2–3.\n857\t- No 3×5 `tiny` font for information.\n858\t- A 5% safe margin from the edges.\n859\t- Information is never in colour alone: aspects also show as lamp *positions* plus a letter.\n860\t- At most 6 HUD groups at once: the Halo ring, the station line, the top-right cluster, the stop gauge, the centre banner (judgments and toasts),\n861\t  and the subtitle box.\n862\t\n863\t**Cab** (screen space; the bottom 30% of r.H; scales to any width):\n864\t- Windshield frame: pillars at the sides, a top header with a sun visor, wipers (rain), faint glass reflections at night.\n865\t- **Dashboard, left to right:**\n866\t  1. **timetable card** (station / ARR / DEP, next stop highlighted, PASS rows) with the **pocket watch** clipped beside it;\n867\t  2. **speedometer** (analog 0–120, needle smoothing, with a big digital readout under it and the ATS lamps: white \"ATS\" power, red \"ATS\");\n868\t  3. **notch display**: a big readout (\"P3\", \"N\", \"B5\", \"EB\") plus a 15-cell strip with the current cell lit, power blue and brake orange;\n869\t  4. **BC/MR pressure gauge**;\n870\t  5. **pilot lamp** (orange), door side arrows, SLIP and WSP lamps, the comfort face;\n871\t  6. **buttons**: ATS confirm, horn, EB (big and round; they depress when used; clickable).\n872\t- The **master controller**: Halo 8000 has one T-handle; Halo 3000 has two handles, brake and power, the brake one moving first. It is drawn in\n873\t  perspective bottom-left; a **white-gloved hand** rests on it and follows its tween (80 ms per notch).\n874\t- Hover with the mouse highlights a widget (focus); click operates it; drag the handle to change notches.\n875\t\n876\t**HUD:**\n877\t- **Top-left: Halo Time ring.** A gold halo of 30 px, filling clockwise as a share of 99, with the seconds inside and \"HALO\" under it.\n878\t- **Top-centre: next station.** Line 1: `NEXT  HANABATAKE` (scale 2). Line 2: `1.25 km · ARR 06:42:05` (or `PASS`).\n879\t- **Top-right:**\n880\t  - the limit in force as a white plate with the number;\n881\t  - the next limit, e.g. \"▸70 in 320 m\";\n882\t  - the next sighted signal as a mini 4-lamp head, its letter and distance;\n883\t  - the clock.\n884\t- **Stop Gauge** (600 m → 0, above the dash, centred):\n885\t  - a horizontal bar with the stop marker and our train's nose, and a big \"123.4 m\" readout;\n886\t  - within 30 m it zooms to a ±2 m scale with 10 cm ticks;\n887\t  - with **Braking guide** (assist) on: an amber \"BRAKE\" zone where B4 must start, `d = v²/(2·aB4) + v·τ`, and a ghost marker at the predicted\n888\t    stop point.\n889\t- **Judgments:** centre screen, `E.font.title` scale 3, colour-coded (PERFECT gold, GREAT cyan, GOOD green, OK white, SHORT orange, OVERRUN red,\n890\t  ON TIME gold, LATE red, SMOOTH pink), held 1.2 s, then a smaller list of the Halo Time changes.\n891\t- **Toasts** (top-centre under the station line): \"HORN +1\", \"CALL GREAT\", \"LIMIT!\", \"ATS!\".\n892\t- **Subtitles:** a box above the dash with the speaker's name in their colour and a portrait for story characters (§4.18).\n893\t\n894\t**Tests:**\n895\t- The HUD state builder (pure function) for given states (approach at 500 m with the guide on, ATS ringing, rain, night).\n896\t- Widget hit boxes map clicks to actions.\n897\t- Element count ≤ 6.\n898\t- Layout at 320×240, 427×240 and 320×690 (upright) keeps everything on screen and out of `game.cornerArea()`.\n899\t\n900\t### 4.15 Input, devices, glyphs, touch-only (`00-core.js`, `05-glyphs.js`)\n901\t\n902\t**Input map** (custom preset):\n903\t```js\n904\tconst KEYS = Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, {\n905\t  power: ['KeyW', 'ArrowUp', 'Pad7', 'Pad12', 'PadAxisUp'],  brake: ['KeyS', 'ArrowDown', 'Pad6', 'Pad13', 'PadAxisDown'],\n906\t  neutral: ['KeyN', 'Pad5'],  eb: ['Backspace', 'KeyX', 'Pad4'],  call: ['KeyE', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0'],  horn: ['Space', 'Pad2'],\n907\t  ats: ['KeyQ', 'Pad3'],  view: ['KeyC', 'Pad1'] });\n908\tfor (const k of ['attack', 'dash', 'skill', 'jump']) delete KEYS[k];\n909\t```\n910\t- The mouse wheel is handled by our own `wheel` listener (browser only), which turns it into `input.tap('power' | 'brake')`.\n911\t- Set the drive scene's `camera: { zoom: false }` so the engine doesn't take the wheel.\n912\t- `look` isn't an action. Read `input.padAim` (the right stick) and `input.pan()` (a right or middle drag).\n913\t\n914\t**Prompt badges** (`DH.Glyph.draw(g, action, x, y, scale)`):\n915\t- The device comes from `input.lastDevice`, and the badge family from the setting (§4.25), not the controller's name.\n916\t- **PlayStation:** a round badge with ✕ (blue), ○ (red), □ (pink), △ (green); pill badges for L1, R1, L2, R2; a D-pad icon with an arrow.\n917\t- **Xbox:** A (green), B (red), X (blue), Y (yellow); LB, RB, LT, RT pills.\n918\t- **Keyboard:** a keycap with the key label from `input.label(a, 'keyboard')` (\"W\", \"SPACE\", \"BKSP\").\n919\t- **Mouse:** a mouse icon with the left or right button lit, or the wheel with an arrow.\n920\t- Prompts change on the step the device changes. The title, menus, results and attract all use them.\n921\t\n922\t**Rumble** (`input.rumble`): rail joints .05/.03 s; EB .8/.4 s; slip .3 buzz; obstruction alarm pulses; PERFECT a short .2 tick.\n923\t\n924\t**Touch-only devices:**\n925\t- Detect `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`, with no gamepad and no key seen.\n926\t- Re-check on `gamepadconnected` and `keydown`.\n927\t- When touch-only:\n928\t  - no touch controls (don't set `touch:` on scenes; `game.input.touchEnabled = false`);\n929\t  - the title replaces its menu with a panel \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a big **SETTINGS** button (`game.menu('settings')`), and the\n930\t    engine's corner menu button stays;\n931\t  - the **attract mode still runs** after 10 s, and a tap returns to the title.\n932\t\n933\t**Tests:**\n934\t- Glyph mapping: PlayStation `call` → ✕; Xbox `call` → A; keyboard `call` → E; mouse `call` → LMB.\n935\t- Lever direction flips W/S but not R2/L2.\n936\t- Wheel taps.\n937\t- Touch-only (stub matchMedia and navigator): the title shows the message; Start can't be reached by taps; attract starts after 10 s; a tap exits it.\n938\t\n939\t### 4.16 Sound: soundscape, sfx, melodies, music (`40-sound.js`)\n940\t\n941\tAll sound is made in code. Use the engine's `game.audio` (sfx, `define`, music, `sting`) for one-shots and songs, and a small **continuous\n942\tsynth** on `audio.ctx` routed through `audio.bus('sfx')` (§3.8) for engine sounds. Build its node graph once; change only parameters each frame.\n943\tIn headless mode every call does nothing.\n944\t\n945\t**Continuous layers** (parameters from speed, notch and zone):\n946\t\n947\t| Layer | Recipe |\n948\t|---|---|\n949\t| **VVVF inverter** (Halo 8000) | carrier: square through a bandpass. 0–12 km/h: an async rising tone 300→900 Hz; then stepped \"synchronous\" bands, each a rising pitch ramp that jumps down at the band edge (bands at 12/24/38/50/65 km/h), the \"singing\" that makes Japanese EMUs famous; plus a motor sine at 18·v Hz; volume ∝ traction/brake effort (regen under braking) |\n950\t| **resistor/motor** (Halo 3000) | low saw at 6·v Hz through a lowpass, a growl that rises with v; a notch-step \"clunk\" each power notch; contactor clicks |\n951\t| rolling | noise through a lowpass at 200 + 12·v Hz, volume ∝ v |\n952\t| wind | noise through a bandpass at 800 Hz, volume ∝ v²; louder in tunnels |\n953\t| tunnel | low rumble boost plus a feedback delay of 60 ms, 0.3 |\n954\t| bridge | hollow girder roar (bandpass 400 Hz) |\n955\t| rain | hiss plus random droplet ticks on the glass; wiper squeak/thunk |\n956\t| brake squeal | sine 2.6–3.0 kHz with vibrato when braking under 8 km/h |\n957\t| compressor | thumps at 3 Hz while stopped, after an air release |\n958\t\n959\t**One-shots:**\n960\t- the train: lever clack, EB clunk plus hiss, brake release \"pshh\" at a full stop, rail-joint \"ta-tan\" (noise through a lowpass; at each 25 m\n961\t  joint, 4 clicks for the bogie spacing), points (rapid clicks);\n962\t- doors: door chime, door thunk, conductor buzzer, pilot lamp click;\n963\t- warnings: ATS bell (repeating ding), ATS chime, slip whine, the halo ring cracking (game over), the clock tick (≤ 10 s);\n964\t- the horn: an air horn of two detuned saws, 349 + 440 Hz, through a lowpass, held while the button is down;\n965\t- crossings: bells alternating 700/820 Hz at 2.5 Hz, with Doppler (pitch × (1 ± v/340)) and distance falloff;\n966\t- other trains: oncoming whoosh (a noise swell plus a toot);\n967\t- ambience: seagulls (sine chirps, harbor), ferry horn (low saw), taiko pattern (festival), cicadas (afternoon noise bursts), crowd murmur (a Babble\n968\t  chorus at low volume);\n969\t- UI: menu move, confirm, back; judgment jingles (PERFECT: a sparkly arpeggio C-E-G-C-E; GREAT; GOOD; OK; OVERRUN: a dissonant minor 2nd).\n970\t\n971\t**Departure melodies** (*hassha* melodies): 12 original 6–8 s tunes, one per station, on a bell timbre (triangle plus sine an octave up, fast decay,\n972\ta light echo).\n973\t- Write each as a note list in the engine's note names (`'E5 . G5 A5 ...'` with a bpm).\n974\t- Play it once by scheduling every note as an sfx voice with its `delay` (sfx voices take `delay`), through the sfx bus, so it works whether or not\n975\t  music is playing.\n976\t- Each should be bright, about 2 bars, and end on a resolving note.\n977\t\n978\t**Music** (engine sequencer; `music(song, { fade: 1 })`):\n979\t\n980\t| song | use |\n981\t|---|---|\n982\t| \"Halo Line March\" | title and attract |\n983\t| \"Timetable\" | menus and halo map (calm city-pop) |\n984\t| \"Briefing\" | soft |\n985\t| \"Results\" | variants S/A, B/C, D |\n986\t| \"Game Over\" | sting |\n987\t| \"Parallel Run\" | lobby and card, energetic |\n988\t| \"Evening Halo\" | ending and credits, gentle |\n989\t| \"Commute Groove\" | optional driving music (setting, off by default; driving is about the soundscape) |\n990\t\n991\t- Duck the music under dialog (`audio.duck`).\n992\t\n993\t**Tests** (headless): the soundscape parameter function (v, notch, zone → layer volumes and frequencies) is pure and tested (VVVF band edges, wind ∝ v²);\n994\tevery named sfx and song exists; a browser test counts active nodes after 60 s of driving (no growing leak).\n995\t\n996\t### 4.17 Voices: Babble, gibberish in the Animalese/Simlish style (`41-babble.js`)\n997\t\n998\t`DH.Babble.say(text, voice, { onSyllable, onDone })` and a per-character hook for Dialog (§3.8 #2).\n999\t\n1000\t**Syllables:**\n1001\t- Each word becomes CV syllables from a **hash of the word**, so the same line always sounds the same.\n1002\t- Japanese-like phonotactics (ka, shi, tsu, ne, ho, ri, n...), about 1 syllable per 2 letters.\n1003\t- Punctuation shapes the pitch: `?` rises, `!` adds emphasis and a fall, `.` drops at the end, `,` adds a short pause; capitals add stress.\n1004\t\n1005\t**Synthesis of each syllable** (60–110 ms):\n1006\t- a consonant burst: noise through a bandpass by consonant class, 15–40 ms;\n1007\t- then a vowel: a pulse or saw source at f0 through two bandpass formants;\n1008\t- formants (Hz): a = 800/1200, i = 300/2300, u = 350/1300, e = 500/1900, o = 500/900, scaled by the voice's `size`;\n1009\t- an envelope with a 6 ms attack and a 30 ms release.\n1010\t- f0 follows a declination contour with slight random jitter from the word hash.\n1011\t\n1012\t**Voices:**\n1013\t\n1014\t| id | who | f0 Hz | rate syl/s | source | character |\n1015\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1016\t| hoshino | Chief Instructor | 95 | 9 | saw | gruff, falling contour |\n1017\t| mika | Conductor | 260 | 13 | pulse 25% | bright, rising ends |\n1018\t| pa | Station announcer | 190 | 10 | pulse | flat and formal, PA bandpass 300–3000 Hz plus a 120 ms echo |\n1019\t| haro | Haro-kun | 420 | 14 | pulse 12% | bouncy, +3 semitone wobble |\n1020\t| driver | the player (calls) | 150 | 11 | square | crisp, loud, clipped |\n1021\t| kota / fuji / kenji / ren / sato / ota | kid / grandma / salaryman / rival / old driver / station master | 340 / 230 / 140 / 165 / 110 / 120 | 12–14 / 8 / 15 / 12 / 8 / 9 | mixed | as in §5.4 |\n1022\t| crowd | murmur | random 120–300 | 10 | mixed | quiet chorus |\n1023\t\n1024\t**Subtitles** are on by default (they carry the meaning). Calls show romaji and English (§4.7).\n1025\t\n1026\t**Tests:** syllabify is deterministic (same text, same syllables) and its length scales with the text; punctuation changes the contour; every voice id\n1027\texists; Dialog calls the voice hook for each character.\n1028\t\n1029\t### 4.18 Story, dialogue, subtitles, tips (`52-story.js`)\n1030\t\n1031\t- **Briefings** (before each Career stage):\n1032\t  - a modal `E.Dialog` with portraits (`portrait: rig`, a Humanoid dressed per §5.4, expressions via `expr`), with Babble voices;\n1033\t  - behind it, the rail renderer shows the stage's first station at its time of day;\n1034\t  - then a **stage card**: name, time, weather icon, train, stops, best rank, and \"START [✕]\".\n1035\t- **Radio chatter** in play: non-modal `talk.say(line, { name, portrait, auto: 3, modal: false })`, so the train keeps running. It is triggered by\n1036\t  `radio` events (§4.11) or by rules (first ATS, first yellow, first slip, Halo Time ≤ 10, a missed call). Lines queue so they never overlap."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619","uuid":"67bf4107-d99d-4b2c-9ebf-3b2e7503ed10","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:33:38.645Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174801__xc8e150/work/PLAN.md","content":"| `radio` | a story line (§4.18) |\n| `tip` | a first-time tutorial tip (once per save, unless \"Show tips\" is on) |\n| `flash` | the oncoming last train flashes its headlights; answer with the horn within 4 s for +2 s (S5) |\n\n**Tests:** each event fires once in its stage under the autopilot playthrough; crossing gates are down while our train is on the crossing; the obstruction\nclears only after we stop; the horn makes workers wave; door rush adds 8 s to that dwell.\n\n### 4.12 Time of day, weather and light (`40-sound.js` for audio, `rail.js` for the image)\n\n**Time of day** is a float hour from the stage (or Free Run). It picks one of these grades, blended linearly between neighbours:\n\n| grade | hours | sky (top → horizon) | ambient | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| dawn | 5–7 | #2a2850 → #f0a080 | .70 warm | low sun east, long shadows (darker ground bands) |\n| day | 8–15 | #3a78d8 → #bfe0ff | 1.0 | |\n| afternoon haze | 14–16 | #5a8ad0 → #e8e0c8 | .95 | |\n| sunset | 17–18.5 | #3a2a6a → #ff9a4a | .75 orange | big low sun on the backdrop; glare zones |\n| night | 19.5–4.5 | #060818 → #1a2440 | .30 blue | stars, moon, city glow; lit windows; headlights; platform lights stay bright |\n\n- The backdrop is an `E.Backdrop` with layers per zone (mountains, hills, city, sea, clouds). Its `turn` scrolls with the curve × distance travelled.\n- **Rain** (S2, Free Run):\n  - darker grade (−20%); streaks and windshield droplets that build up;\n  - wipers sweep every 1.2 s in the cab;\n  - wet rails; puddles on platforms (bright blots);\n  - umbrellas on the crowds;\n  - rain hiss in the audio, and lower adhesion (§4.3).\n- **Fog:** distance fog, larger signal glows, short sighting. Hoshino: \"When you can't see, the signals see for you.\"\n- **Glare:** an additive white-orange bloom over the upper half, scaled by `cos(heading − sunHeading)`. The sun visor animates down.\n\n**Tests:** the grade blend is continuous at every boundary hour; rain sets the adhesion; fog sets sighting to 180 m.\n\n### 4.13 Cameras and the director (`60-drive.js`, `61-attract.js`)\n\n| camera | placement | use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cab` | s_front − 1.5, x = track, h 2.9, horizon .40 | gameplay (default) |\n| `cabMin` | the same, without the dashboard | \"Minimal\" cab display setting; duel viewports |\n| `chase` | behind the rear by 25 m, h 9, x +2, fov 70 | View button; attract |\n| `chaseHigh` | rear − 40 m, h 22, x +6 | quad-track shots, the hub card |\n| `platform` | on the platform 15 m past the stop target, h 1.7, `dir: -1` | View button at stations; attract; the S5 finale |\n| `trackside` | 4 m beside the line at a scripted s ahead, h 2, `dir: -1`; holds while the train passes, then cuts | attract, ending |\n| `stopCam` | 2D side view (§4.6) | after every stop |\n\n- `view` cycles through cab → chase → platform (platform only near a station) → cab.\n- **Cab feel:**\n  - lateral sway ∝ curve × v²;\n  - 1-px vertical bumps at rail joints (every 25 m, timed with the joint sound);\n  - the view pitches 2–3 px forward under braking and back under power (`pitchPx` follows the smoothed acceleration);\n  - a jolt on EB.\n  - The \"Camera sway\" setting scales these; reduce motion turns them off.\n- **Look:** the right stick or a right-drag shifts `lookX` up to ±25% of W and `lookY` ±10% of H, springing back. It's a glance, not a free camera.\n- **Director** (attract, ending): a list of shots `{ cam, dur, at }`, cut with 0.3 s dissolves.\n\n**Tests:** each camera projects our train's front to the expected screen side; `view` cycles only through the cameras that apply; the director runs\nits shots in order.\n\n### 4.14 Cab, HUD, stop gauge (`50-cab.js`, `51-hud.js`)\n\n**10-foot rules:**\n- Nothing smaller than the 5×7 font at scale 1. Key numbers (speed, stop distance, Halo Time, notch) at scale 2–3.\n- No 3×5 `tiny` font for information.\n- A 5% safe margin from the edges.\n- Information is never in colour alone: aspects also show as lamp *positions* plus a letter.\n- At most 6 HUD groups at once: the Halo ring, the station line, the top-right cluster, the stop gauge, the centre banner (judgments and toasts),\n  and the subtitle box.\n\n**Cab** (screen space; the bottom 30% of r.H; scales to any width):\n- Windshield frame: pillars at the sides, a top header with a sun visor, wipers (rain), faint glass reflections at night.\n- **Dashboard, left to right:**\n  1. **timetable card** (station / ARR / DEP, next stop highlighted, PASS rows) with the **pocket watch** clipped beside it;\n  2. **speedometer** (analog 0–120, needle smoothing, with a big digital readout under it and the ATS lamps: white \"ATS\" power, red \"ATS\");\n  3. **notch display**: a big readout (\"P3\", \"N\", \"B5\", \"EB\") plus a 15-cell strip with the current cell lit, power blue and brake orange;\n  4. **BC/MR pressure gauge**;\n  5. **pilot lamp** (orange), door side arrows, SLIP and WSP lamps, the comfort face;\n  6. **buttons**: ATS confirm, horn, EB (big and round; they depress when used; clickable).\n- The **master controller**: Halo 8000 has one T-handle; Halo 3000 has two handles, brake and power, the brake one moving first. It is drawn in\n  perspective bottom-left; a **white-gloved hand** rests on it and follows its tween (80 ms per notch).\n- Hover with the mouse highlights a widget (focus); click operates it; drag the handle to change notches.\n\n**HUD:**\n- **Top-left: Halo Time ring.** A gold halo of 30 px, filling clockwise as a share of 99, with the seconds inside and \"HALO\" under it.\n- **Top-centre: next station.** Line 1: `NEXT  HANABATAKE` (scale 2). Line 2: `1.25 km · ARR 06:42:05` (or `PASS`).\n- **Top-right:**\n  - the limit in force as a white plate with the number;\n  - the next limit, e.g. \"▸70 in 320 m\";\n  - the next sighted signal as a mini 4-lamp head, its letter and distance;\n  - the clock.\n- **Stop Gauge** (600 m → 0, above the dash, centred):\n  - a horizontal bar with the stop marker and our train's nose, and a big \"123.4 m\" readout;\n  - within 30 m it zooms to a ±2 m scale with 10 cm ticks;\n  - with **Braking guide** (assist) on: an amber \"BRAKE\" zone where B4 must start, `d = v²/(2·aB4) + v·τ`, and a ghost marker at the predicted\n    stop point.\n- **Judgments:** centre screen, `E.font.title` scale 3, colour-coded (PERFECT gold, GREAT cyan, GOOD green, OK white, SHORT orange, OVERRUN red,\n  ON TIME gold, LATE red, SMOOTH pink), held 1.2 s, then a smaller list of the Halo Time changes.\n- **Toasts** (top-centre under the station line): \"HORN +1\", \"CALL GREAT\", \"LIMIT!\", \"ATS!\".\n- **Subtitles:** a box above the dash with the speaker's name in their colour and a portrait for story characters (§4.18).\n\n**Tests:**\n- The HUD state builder (pure function) for given states (approach at 500 m with the guide on, ATS ringing, rain, night).\n- Widget hit boxes map clicks to actions.\n- Element count ≤ 6.\n- Layout at 320×240, 427×240 and 320×690 (upright) keeps everything on screen and out of `game.cornerArea()`.\n\n### 4.15 Input, devices, glyphs, touch-only (`00-core.js`, `05-glyphs.js`)\n\n**Input map** (custom preset):\n```js\nconst KEYS = Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, {\n  power: ['KeyW', 'ArrowUp', 'Pad7', 'Pad12', 'PadAxisUp'],  brake: ['KeyS', 'ArrowDown', 'Pad6', 'Pad13', 'PadAxisDown'],\n  neutral: ['KeyN', 'Pad5'],  eb: ['Backspace', 'KeyX', 'Pad4'],  call: ['KeyE', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0'],  horn: ['Space', 'Pad2'],\n  ats: ['KeyQ', 'Pad3'],  view: ['KeyC', 'Pad1'] });\nfor (const k of ['attack', 'dash', 'skill', 'jump']) delete KEYS[k];\n```\n- The mouse wheel is handled by our own `wheel` listener (browser only), which turns it into `input.tap('power' | 'brake')`.\n- Set the drive scene's `camera: { zoom: false }` so the engine doesn't take the wheel.\n- `look` isn't an action. Read `input.padAim` (the right stick) and `input.pan()` (a right or middle drag).\n\n**Prompt badges** (`DH.Glyph.draw(g, action, x, y, scale)`):\n- The device comes from `input.lastDevice`, and the badge family from the setting (§4.25), not the controller's name.\n- **PlayStation:** a round badge with ✕ (blue), ○ (red), □ (pink), △ (green); pill badges for L1, R1, L2, R2; a D-pad icon with an arrow.\n- **Xbox:** A (green), B (red), X (blue), Y (yellow); LB, RB, LT, RT pills.\n- **Keyboard:** a keycap with the key label from `input.label(a, 'keyboard')` (\"W\", \"SPACE\", \"BKSP\").\n- **Mouse:** a mouse icon with the left or right button lit, or the wheel with an arrow.\n- Prompts change on the step the device changes. The title, menus, results and attract all use them.\n\n**Rumble** (`input.rumble`): rail joints .05/.03 s; EB .8/.4 s; slip .3 buzz; obstruction alarm pulses; PERFECT a short .2 tick.\n\n**Touch-only devices:**\n- Detect `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`, with no gamepad and no key seen.\n- Re-check on `gamepadconnected` and `keydown`.\n- When touch-only:\n  - no touch controls (don't set `touch:` on scenes; `game.input.touchEnabled = false`);\n  - the title replaces its menu with a panel \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a big **SETTINGS** button (`game.menu('settings')`), and the\n    engine's corner menu button stays;\n  - the **attract mode still runs** after 10 s, and a tap returns to the title.\n\n**Tests:**\n- Glyph mapping: PlayStation `call` → ✕; Xbox `call` → A; keyboard `call` → E; mouse `call` → LMB.\n- Lever direction flips W/S but not R2/L2.\n- Wheel taps.\n- Touch-only (stub matchMedia and navigator): the title shows the message; Start can't be reached by taps; attract starts after 10 s; a tap exits it.\n\n### 4.16 Sound: soundscape, sfx, melodies, music (`40-sound.js`)\n\nAll sound is made in code. Use the engine's `game.audio` (sfx, `define`, music, `sting`) for one-shots and songs, and a small **continuous\nsynth** on `audio.ctx` routed through `audio.bus('sfx')` (§3.8) for engine sounds. Build its node graph once; change only parameters each frame.\nIn headless mode every call does nothing.\n\n**Continuous layers** (parameters from speed, notch and zone):\n\n| Layer | Recipe |\n|---|---|\n| **VVVF inverter** (Halo 8000) | carrier: square through a bandpass. 0–12 km/h: an async rising tone 300→900 Hz; then stepped \"synchronous\" bands, each a rising pitch ramp that jumps down at the band edge (bands at 12/24/38/50/65 km/h), the \"singing\" that makes Japanese EMUs famous; plus a motor sine at 18·v Hz; volume ∝ traction/brake effort (regen under braking) |\n| **resistor/motor** (Halo 3000) | low saw at 6·v Hz through a lowpass, a growl that rises with v; a notch-step \"clunk\" each power notch; contactor clicks |\n| rolling | noise through a lowpass at 200 + 12·v Hz, volume ∝ v |\n| wind | noise through a bandpass at 800 Hz, volume ∝ v²; louder in tunnels |\n| tunnel | low rumble boost plus a feedback delay of 60 ms, 0.3 |\n| bridge | hollow girder roar (bandpass 400 Hz) |\n| rain | hiss plus random droplet ticks on the glass; wiper squeak/thunk |\n| brake squeal | sine 2.6–3.0 kHz with vibrato when braking under 8 km/h |\n| compressor | thumps at 3 Hz while stopped, after an air release |\n\n**One-shots:**\n- the train: lever clack, EB clunk plus hiss, brake release \"pshh\" at a full stop, rail-joint \"ta-tan\" (noise through a lowpass; at each 25 m\n  joint, 4 clicks for the bogie spacing), points (rapid clicks);\n- doors: door chime, door thunk, conductor buzzer, pilot lamp click;\n- warnings: ATS bell (repeating ding), ATS chime, slip whine, the halo ring cracking (game over), the clock tick (≤ 10 s);\n- the horn: an air horn of two detuned saws, 349 + 440 Hz, through a lowpass, held while the button is down;\n- crossings: bells alternating 700/820 Hz at 2.5 Hz, with Doppler (pitch × (1 ± v/340)) and distance falloff;\n- other trains: oncoming whoosh (a noise swell plus a toot);\n- ambience: seagulls (sine chirps, harbor), ferry horn (low saw), taiko pattern (festival), cicadas (afternoon noise bursts), crowd murmur (a Babble\n  chorus at low volume);\n- UI: menu move, confirm, back; judgment jingles (PERFECT: a sparkly arpeggio C-E-G-C-E; GREAT; GOOD; OK; OVERRUN: a dissonant minor 2nd).\n\n**Departure melodies** (*hassha* melodies): 12 original 6–8 s tunes, one per station, on a bell timbre (triangle plus sine an octave up, fast decay,\na light echo).\n- Write each as a note list in the engine's note names (`'E5 . G5 A5 ...'` with a bpm).\n- Play it once by scheduling every note as an sfx voice with its `delay` (sfx voices take `delay`), through the sfx bus, so it works whether or not\n  music is playing.\n- Each should be bright, about 2 bars, and end on a resolving note.\n\n**Music** (engine sequencer; `music(song, { fade: 1 })`):\n\n| song | use |\n|---|---|\n| \"Halo Line March\" | title and attract |\n| \"Timetable\" | menus and halo map (calm city-pop) |\n| \"Briefing\" | soft |\n| \"Results\" | variants S/A, B/C, D |\n| \"Game Over\" | sting |\n| \"Parallel Run\" | lobby and card, energetic |\n| \"Evening Halo\" | ending and credits, gentle |\n| \"Commute Groove\" | optional driving music (setting, off by default; driving is about the soundscape) |\n\n- Duck the music under dialog (`audio.duck`).\n\n**Tests** (headless): the soundscape parameter function (v, notch, zone → layer volumes and frequencies) is pure and tested (VVVF band edges, wind ∝ v²);\nevery named sfx and song exists; a browser test counts active nodes after 60 s of driving (no growing leak).\n\n### 4.17 Voices: Babble, gibberish in the Animalese/Simlish style (`41-babble.js`)\n\n`DH.Babble.say(text, voice, { onSyllable, onDone })` and a per-character hook for Dialog (§3.8 #2).\n\n**Syllables:**\n- Each word becomes CV syllables from a **hash of the word**, so the same line always sounds the same.\n- Japanese-like phonotactics (ka, shi, tsu, ne, ho, ri, n...), about 1 syllable per 2 letters.\n- Punctuation shapes the pitch: `?` rises, `!` adds emphasis and a fall, `.` drops at the end, `,` adds a short pause; capitals add stress.\n\n**Synthesis of each syllable** (60–110 ms):\n- a consonant burst: noise through a bandpass by consonant class, 15–40 ms;\n- then a vowel: a pulse or saw source at f0 through two bandpass formants;\n- formants (Hz): a = 800/1200, i = 300/2300, u = 350/1300, e = 500/1900, o = 500/900, scaled by the voice's `size`;\n- an envelope with a 6 ms attack and a 30 ms release.\n- f0 follows a declination contour with slight random jitter from the word hash.\n\n**Voices:**\n\n| id | who | f0 Hz | rate syl/s | source | character |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| hoshino | Chief Instructor | 95 | 9 | saw | gruff, falling contour |\n| mika | Conductor | 260 | 13 | pulse 25% | bright, rising ends |\n| pa | Station announcer | 190 | 10 | pulse | flat and formal, PA bandpass 300–3000 Hz plus a 120 ms echo |\n| haro | Haro-kun | 420 | 14 | pulse 12% | bouncy, +3 semitone wobble |\n| driver | the player (calls) | 150 | 11 | square | crisp, loud, clipped |\n| kota / fuji / kenji / ren / sato / ota | kid / grandma / salaryman / rival / old driver / station master | 340 / 230 / 140 / 165 / 110 / 120 | 12–14 / 8 / 15 / 12 / 8 / 9 | mixed | as in §5.4 |\n| crowd | murmur | random 120–300 | 10 | mixed | quiet chorus |\n\n**Subtitles** are on by default (they carry the meaning). Calls show romaji and English (§4.7).\n\n**Tests:** syllabify is deterministic (same text, same syllables) and its length scales with the text; punctuation changes the contour; every voice id\nexists; Dialog calls the voice hook for each character.\n\n### 4.18 Story, dialogue, subtitles, tips (`52-story.js`)\n\n- **Briefings** (before each Career stage):\n  - a modal `E.Dialog` with portraits (`portrait: rig`, a Humanoid dressed per §5.4, expressions via `expr`), with Babble voices;\n  - behind it, the rail renderer shows the stage's first station at its time of day;\n  - then a **stage card**: name, time, weather icon, train, stops, best rank, and \"START [✕]\".\n- **Radio chatter** in play: non-modal `talk.say(line, { name, portrait, auto: 3, modal: false })`, so the train keeps running. It is triggered by\n  `radio` events (§4.11) or by rules (first ATS, first yellow, first slip, Halo Time ≤ 10, a missed call). Lines queue so they never overlap.","numLines":240,"startLine":797,"totalLines":1945}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Mc3c14zGZpStuPanPjVY78","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2WtNbQZTF4ekYndVDJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0131hCArUijFiY6kSmtV7Dgm","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174801__xc8e150/work/PLAN.md","offset":1037,"limit":260},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8108,"cache_read_input_tokens":430715,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8108},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619","uuid":"d6baff5c-effc-4ef0-81d5-08d3ffbd4000","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:33:40.059Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2Wt8TQ2ZnVtsqvFxRD","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0131hCArUijFiY6kSmtV7Dgm":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174801__xc8e150/work/PLAN.md","offset":1037,"limit":260}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0131hCArUijFiY6kSmtV7Dgm","type":"tool_result","content":"1037\t- **Tips:** the first time each mechanic appears, show a short tip toast with the glyph (\"ATS! Press [△] to confirm, then brake.\"). They are\n1038\t  remembered in `E.store`, and the \"Show tips\" setting brings them back.\n1039\t- **Results lines** by rank (§5.5) and **game over** lines per stage.\n1040\t- All text lives in `22-stages.js` as data. The style rules are in §5.5.\n1041\t\n1042\t**Tests:** every stage has a briefing and results lines for S/A/B/C/D; radio lines queue; tips show once per save; no line is longer than 3 rows at 320 px\n1043\twidth (`E.font.wrap`).\n1044\t\n1045\t### 4.19 Autopilot (`35-autopilot.js`): used by attract, Dev auto-drive, tests and calibration\n1046\t\n1047\t**Planner.** Over the next 1,500 m, take the constraints:\n1048\t- line speed;\n1049\t- limit boards (taking train length into account);\n1050\t- signal-derived limits (Y 45 and so on);\n1051\t- red signals: a stop target 25 m before;\n1052\t- the stop target at the next stopping station;\n1053\t- slow orders;\n1054\t- the obstruction (when the emitter is flashing).\n1055\t\n1056\t**Allowed speed:** `v_allow(d) = sqrt(v_c² + 2·a_plan·max(0, d − v·τ))`, where a_plan = 0.8 × B4 decel. Target v* = min over constraints minus 2 km/h.\n1057\t\n1058\t**Control:**\n1059\t- If v < v* − 4, power: the notch is chosen from the speed gap.\n1060\t- If v > v* + 1, brake with notch `ceil(req / 0.5)`, where `req = (v² − v_c²) / (2·d)` (in matched units).\n1061\t- **Final stop:** model-predictive. Simulate the train model 12 s ahead for each candidate brake notch, and choose the one whose predicted stop point\n1062\t  is closest to the target. Inside the last 2 m it eases to B1 (smooth stop). This should hit PERFECT reliably. If it doesn't, the physics or the\n1063\t  lag model is wrong; fix that, not the bot.\n1064\t\n1065\t**Timetable (needed for ON TIME and rank S):**\n1066\t- Every 2 s, pick a cruise cap by bisection: for each candidate, simulate the train model over the rest of the interval (0.1 s steps, with the\n1067\t  same constraints) and keep the cap whose predicted arrival matches the scheduled `arr` (aim for −0.5 s).\n1068\t- Coast (N) where that still arrives on time.\n1069\t- Depart the moment the departure is allowed.\n1070\t- **Min-time mode** ignores the timetable and drives as fast as the rules allow. This is \"par\" for calibration (§4.8).\n1071\t\n1072\t**Procedures:** confirm ATS within 0.5 s; make every call at the sweet spot; horn for 0.8 s at each horn board; EB on the obstruction emitter if it can't\n1073\tstop with B8; answer the S5 flash.\n1074\t\n1075\t**Adhesion:** in rain, never pick a power or brake notch whose effort exceeds the adhesion limit (B5 at most with the default 2.6 km/h/s). Plan the\n1076\tbraking with that notch, so the S2 run has no slip or slide.\n1077\t\n1078\t**Skill** (0–1, a dev slider):\n1079\t- 1 = perfect;\n1080\t- 0.6 = human-like: 0.25 s reaction delay, ±3 km/h noise, stop aim ±0.4 m, drawn from `DH.rng('auto')`.\n1081\t\n1082\t**Cheat flags** (attract): no Halo Time loss; teleport between vignettes; forced events.\n1083\t\n1084\t**Tests:**\n1085\t- Skill 1 completes every stage with every stop GOOD or better (PERFECT on ≥ 80% of stops), no game over, every scripted event fired, rank S.\n1086\t- Skill 0.6 completes every stage with no game over on Normal.\n1087\t- Its runs are deterministic: proof hash twice.\n1088\t\n1089\t### 4.20 Attract mode (`61-attract.js`)\n1090\t\n1091\t**Trigger:** 10 s with no input on **title, haloMap, freeRun setup, duelLobby, driversLog, help or about**.\n1092\t- Input means any key, gamepad button or stick past the deadzone, mouse move over 4 px, click, wheel or touch.\n1093\t- The timer is game time measured in those scenes. It is paused while a Shell screen (settings) is open, and restarts on any input.\n1094\t\n1095\t**Loop**, about 3 min, using the autopilot with cheats and the camera director:\n1096\t1. **S0 Depot, dawn:** handle close-ups in the cab; a perfect stop at Mark 1, then the Stop Cam.\n1097\t2. **S1 Hanabatake departure:** platform cam showing the doors, the melody, the buzzer and the pilot lamp; the \"Shuppatsu shinko!\" call with the glove;\n1098\t   then the horn board, the oncoming train and the chase cam onto the bridge.\n1099\t3. **S2 rain:** wipers, the SLIP lamp, ATS bell and confirm, yellow signals, the tunnel mouth, passing Todai-shita alongside the freight.\n1100\t4. **S3:** the obstruction crossing (trackside cam, then EB), the track workers waving, a chase cam on the climb.\n1101\t5. **S4:** chaseHigh with the Sky Liner alongside, the door rush (platform cam), sunset glare.\n1102\t6. **S5:** night headlights, fog, the final approach to Akari Central, the staff bow, PERFECT.\n1103\t7. **\"TODAY'S BEST DRIVERS\"** table (top 5 career totals from `E.store`, or seeded defaults with names like \"HOSHINO\"), then the logo card.\n1104\t   Then loop.\n1105\t\n1106\t**Overlay:** a \"DEMO\" badge, a small logo, and \"Press any button\" with the device glyph. On touch-only devices it reads \"Controller or keyboard\n1107\trequired\" instead.\n1108\t\n1109\t**Rules:** any input goes back to the title (`game.go('title', null, { fade: 'wipe' })`) and resets the idle timer. Attract never writes records or\n1110\tpostcards. It is never shown in hub sessions.\n1111\t\n1112\t**Tests** (headless):\n1113\t- `advance(10.2)` on the title enters attract.\n1114\t- The vignettes advance in order; after the loop's length it is back at vignette 1.\n1115\t- `tap('confirm')`, `press('KeyA')`, `feed('move')` and a stick tap each return to the title.\n1116\t- No records are written.\n1117\t- Touch-only still starts attract.\n1118\t\n1119\t### 4.21 Dev Mode (`62-dev.js`)\n1120\t\n1121\t**Reach:** the pause menu item **Dev Mode** (`menu.items`). It is not offered in hub sessions. It opens an `E.Shell` screen with tool buttons and two\n1122\tsub-screens. Everything works with controller, keyboard and mouse.\n1123\t\n1124\t**Tools:**\n1125\t- Auto-drive on/off; autopilot skill;\n1126\t- God mode (no penalties); freeze Halo Time; time scale (0.25×–4×);\n1127\t- jump: to the next station approach (600 m out), to km (a km slider plus Go), skip to the next stop (arrive PERFECT), finish the stage (perfect),\n1128\t  fail the stage (game over);\n1129\t- time of day (slider, 0–24 h); weather (Clear / Rain / Fog);\n1130\t- force the next signal's aspect (R / Y / YG / G);\n1131\t- trigger an event (ATS test, obstruction, door rush, oncoming, workers, flash);\n1132\t- camera (cab / chase / chaseHigh / platform / trackside);\n1133\t- debug overlay on/off (also the backquote key while Dev Mode is unlocked);\n1134\t- braking guide on/off;\n1135\t- unlock all stages; reset save data (asks first, in-game);\n1136\t- **sound board**: play any sfx, melody, song or Babble voice, to audition them.\n1137\t\n1138\t**Balance sliders** (live; the `Dev` group, §3.8 #3), with **Reset to defaults** (`game.settings.reset('Dev')`):\n1139\t\n1140\t| key | label | default | range |\n1141\t|---|---|---|---|\n1142\t| `accelScale` | Power acceleration | 1.0 | 0.5–2.0 |\n1143\t| `brakeScale` | Service brake strength | 1.0 | 0.5–2.0 |\n1144\t| `brakeLag` | Brake response lag (s) | 0.7 | 0.0–2.0 |\n1145\t| `ebDecel` | Emergency brake (km/h/s) | 5.0 | 3.0–7.0 |\n1146\t| `adhesionRain` | Rain adhesion (km/h/s) | 2.6 | 1.0–6.0 |\n1147\t| `gradeScale` | Gradient effect | 1.0 | 0–2 |\n1148\t| `resistScale` | Running resistance | 1.0 | 0–2 |\n1149\t| `graceStart` | Starting Halo Time (s) | 25 | 5–99 |\n1150\t| `lateCost` | Halo Time lost per second late | 1.0 | 0–3 |\n1151\t| `overrunCost` | Seconds lost per metre overrun | 2.0 | 0–5 |\n1152\t| `stopWindow` | Stop window scale | 1.0 | 0.5–3 |\n1153\t| `onTimeWindow` | On-time window (±s) | 2 | 0–10 |\n1154\t| `dwell` | Minimum dwell (s) | 12 | 5–60 |\n1155\t| `callGreat` | Call sweet spot far edge (m) | 120 | 60–200 |\n1156\t| `limitTol` | Speed tolerance (km/h) | 3 | 0–10 |\n1157\t| `atsTime` | ATS confirm time (s) | 5 | 2–10 |\n1158\t| `comfortSens` | Comfort sensitivity | 1.0 | 0–3 |\n1159\t| `sway` | Camera sway | 1.0 | 0–2 |\n1160\t| `fov` | Cab field of view (°) | 75 | 60–90 |\n1161\t\n1162\t- Every system reads `DH.TUNING` each step, so changes apply at once.\n1163\t- **Debug overlay** (pixel text, top-left):\n1164\t  - performance: FPS, draw ms;\n1165\t  - position: s/km, segment and zone;\n1166\t  - train: v (km/h, m/s), a, jerk, notch, P/B effective, adhesion state;\n1167\t  - signals: the next 3 signals with their blocks; the preceding train's s;\n1168\t  - rules: the limit in force, the next stop distance, the autopilot's predicted stop point, Halo Time, score, comfort;\n1169\t  - the event queue, stage, seed.\n1170\t\n1171\t**Tests:** each slider changes behaviour live (doubling `brakeScale` shortens the B4 distance by about 45–55%); Reset restores every default; Dev isn't in the\n1172\tstandard Settings list; Dev Mode is missing from the pause menu in a session; each tool does what it says (jump puts s within 1 m; finishing a stage\n1173\treaches results).\n1174\t\n1175\t### 4.22 Progress, Driver's Log, postcards (`55-ui-log.js`)\n1176\t\n1177\t- **`E.store`** keys: `dh:progress` (`{ unlocked, ranks, best, stats, postcards }`), `dh:tips`, `dh:board` (the attract table). In a hub session the\n1178\t  client's storage is memory-only, so nothing persists. That is intended.\n1179\t- The title's **CAREER** opens the halo map with the next unfinished stage selected.\n1180\t- **Driver's Log** (large cards, no dense tables):\n1181\t  1. **Records:** per stage, the best rank stamp, score, best stop error and perfect count.\n1182\t  2. **Postcards:** 8 cards in a 4×2 grid. Each is a rendered miniature of its scene (the rail renderer drawn into a 160×100 buffer) with a caption.\n1183\t     Selecting one shows it large with a handwritten-style thank-you note.\n1184\t  3. **Stats:** 4 big numbers: km driven, stops made, perfect stops, calls made.\n1185\t\n1186\t| # | Postcard | Condition |\n1187\t|---|---|---|\n1188\t| 1 | \"Your First Halo\" from Haro-kun | any PERFECT stop |\n1189\t| 2 | \"Right at My Door!\" from Kota | S1: Hanabatake GREAT or better |\n1190\t| 3 | \"Rain or Shine\" | S2 with no overrun and no slide |\n1191\t| 4 | \"Takoyaki Saved\" | S3: obstruction avoided |\n1192\t| 5 | \"Not One Second\" | S4: every arrival ON TIME |\n1193\t| 6 | \"The Last Halo\" from Hoshino | finish S5 |\n1194\t| 7 | \"Point, Call, Proceed\" | every call in a stage made GREAT |\n1195\t| 8 | \"Gentle Hands\" | every stop in a stage SMOOTH |\n1196\t\n1197\t**Tests:** each condition unlocks its card (driven by `debug`); progress round-trips through `E.store`; unlocks are in order; attract doesn't unlock\n1198\tanything.\n1199\t\n1200\t### 4.23 Multiplayer A: AI Hub session, **ghost** mode (`63-hub.js`)\n1201\t\n1202\t**Head tags** (after `<meta charset>` and the pasted, unmodified `session.js` script):\n1203\t```html\n1204\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n1205\t<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-2\">\n1206\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n1207\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"2-4\">\n1208\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n1209\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"5\">\n1210\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Best driving score: stop on the mark, on time\">\n1211\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Lever W/S or L2/R2 · Call E/✕ · Horn Space/□ · ATS Q/△\">\n1212\t```\n1213\t(`hub:type` and `hub:players` are catalogue tags copied from the hub examples; `hub:players` is the local count. The goal is ≤ 60 characters and\n1214\tthe howto ≤ 80.)\n1215\t\n1216\t**Flow** (the round is the **Parallel Run**, §5.3):\n1217\t1. `AIHub.active` is true from the first line, so skip the title, attract and menus. Run the boot steps (no key press), then\n1218\t   `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [2, 4], card: true })`.\n1219\t   - If it returns `null`, play normally.\n1220\t2. Build the Parallel Run with `DH.rng` seeded from `AIHub.rng('route')`. Assign tracks by seat: id 0 → x 0, 1 → +7, 2 → −7, 3 → +14 (all four\n1221\t   tracks run clockwise for the festival race). Then call **`AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press.**\n1222\t3. **Our own card** (`hubCard` scene), drawn with the rail renderer's `chaseHigh` camera over the four trains at Shotengai:\n1223\t   - the title;\n1224\t   - \"PARALLEL RUN: four drivers, four tracks, one timetable\";\n1225\t   - the goal; this player's controls (glyphs);\n1226\t   - each player in their seat colour (names drawn with the pixel font, never put in HTML) with ready ticks;\n1227\t   - the time left from the `card` event.\n1228\t   - **Enter, ✕ or a click on READY** → `AIHub.answer('ready')`. **△, T or a click on MORE TIME** → `AIHub.answer('more')` (pressing again takes it\n1229\t     back).\n1230\t   - No other choices, and no `Math.random` on the card.\n1231\t4. On `start`: the 3 s countdown is the Shotengai departure melody. At `AIHub.time() >= 0` the starting signal clears and the round begins.\n1232\t5. **During play:**\n1233\t   - every 100 ms: `AIHub.send({ t: 'g', area: 'quad', s, v, n: notch, st: phase }, { reliable: false })` (10 msgs/s, under the 20/s limit);\n1234\t   - every 1 s: `AIHub.progress({ score, label: 'DAIGAKU · 4,350' })`;\n1235\t   - on each stop: `AIHub.send({ t: 'j', st, j: 'GREAT', e: 0.21 })` (reliable), which shows as a rival toast (\"BLUE: GREAT STOP\").\n1236\t6. **Ghost trains:** the other players' trains are drawn on their tracks at interpolated s (smoothed toward the target, extrapolated by v). Each has\n1237\t   its stripe in the player's colour and a name tag over its front within 400 m. They never affect our world: no blocks, no signals. A small\n1238\t   standings panel lists the players' scores.\n1239\t7. **Finish:** after the last stop's judgment, call `AIHub.result({ score, stats: { 'Perfect stops': n, 'On time': m }, awards: [...] })` **once**,\n1240\t   send `{ t: 'done', score }`, and show \"Waiting for the others\" with live standings.\n1241\t8. **`time-up`:** if not finished, call `AIHub.result({ score })` at once.\n1242\t9. **`leave`:** remove that ghost.\n1243\t10. **`end`:** stop input and all sound (music off, the continuous synth to 0, mute) and show the final standings. The hub then removes the game.\n1244\t\n1245\t**Session rules:**\n1246\t- The pause menu shows only Resume, Settings and Controls: no Quit, Retry or Dev Mode.\n1247\t- No `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt` or `window.open`.\n1248\t- No network except the hub.\n1249\t- No chat or emotes.\n1250\t- Options is ignored while Create is held (§3.8 #7).\n1251\t- Storage is memory-only (the client handles this).\n1252\t- `AIHub.fail(msg)` if the canvas can't be created.\n1253\t\n1254\t**Tests:**\n1255\t- A **mock AIHub** in the Node test (`test/hub-mock.mjs`) checks:\n1256\t  - connect, then ready is called with no input;\n1257\t  - play is gated until `time() >= 0`;\n1258\t  - progress comes at ≥ 1/s;\n1259\t  - the send rate is ≤ 20/s and every payload < 16 KB;\n1260\t  - `result` is called exactly once (finish or time-up);\n1261\t  - `end` silences the game and blocks input;\n1262\t  - no title or attract scene is ever entered;\n1263\t  - the Options-with-Create rule holds.\n1264\t- The **real harness** (§8.4): 2 players with auto; 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; and `scenario=host-leave`. Each must reach\n1265\t  `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`.\n1266\t\n1267\t### 4.24 Multiplayer B: local Duel, split screen (`64-duel.js`)\n1268\t\n1269\t- **Lobby:** P1 is in. \"P2: press START on a second controller, or 2 on the keyboard\" (`game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin })`). Show each half's keys\n1270\t  (§2). The race starts when both press confirm.\n1271\t- **Race:** the Parallel Run at noon; P1 on x 0, P2 on x +7. `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'auto' })`.\n1272\t  - Each viewport: the `cabMin` camera, a compact HUD (speed, notch, stop gauge, Halo Time, score).\n1273\t  - `scene.hud(r)`: the shared clock and the leader.\n1274\t  - Each player sees the other as a real train on the next track.\n1275\t  - Prompts follow each player's own device.\n1276\t- **Results:** both score cards side by side, then the winner's banner with Haro-kun cheering. Rematch / Title.\n1277\t\n1278\t**Tests:** `game.addPlayer({ keys: 'right' })`; both inputs drive their own trains; two viewports draw; two autopilots finish; the winner is the higher score;\n1279\ta tie is shown as a tie.\n1280\t\n1281\t### 4.25 Settings, accessibility, difficulty\n1282\t\n1283\t**The game's own settings** (group `Game`, via `menu.settings` or Shell items):\n1284\t- Difficulty: Easy / Normal / Expert.\n1285\t- **Button symbols: PlayStation / Xbox.** The default is PlayStation; its `apply` sets the standard `glyphs` setting.\n1286\t- Lever direction: Up = Power / Up = Brake.\n1287\t- Braking guide: On / Off (default On for Easy, Off otherwise).\n1288\t- Auto calls: Off / On.\n1289\t- Subtitles: On / Off (default On).\n1290\t- Show tips: On / Off.\n1291\t- Stop Cam: On / Off.\n1292\t- Cab display: Full / Minimal.\n1293\t- Driving music: Off / On.\n1294\t- Camera sway: 0–100%.\n1295\t\n1296\t**Standard settings** (built in; don't rebuild them): volume, music, effects, reduce motion (turns off sway and flashes), screen filter (CRT/scanlines),"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619","uuid":"4b1dabed-e330-4bda-913f-06112c785979","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:33:40.064Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174801__xc8e150/work/PLAN.md","content":"- **Tips:** the first time each mechanic appears, show a short tip toast with the glyph (\"ATS! Press [△] to confirm, then brake.\"). They are\n  remembered in `E.store`, and the \"Show tips\" setting brings them back.\n- **Results lines** by rank (§5.5) and **game over** lines per stage.\n- All text lives in `22-stages.js` as data. The style rules are in §5.5.\n\n**Tests:** every stage has a briefing and results lines for S/A/B/C/D; radio lines queue; tips show once per save; no line is longer than 3 rows at 320 px\nwidth (`E.font.wrap`).\n\n### 4.19 Autopilot (`35-autopilot.js`): used by attract, Dev auto-drive, tests and calibration\n\n**Planner.** Over the next 1,500 m, take the constraints:\n- line speed;\n- limit boards (taking train length into account);\n- signal-derived limits (Y 45 and so on);\n- red signals: a stop target 25 m before;\n- the stop target at the next stopping station;\n- slow orders;\n- the obstruction (when the emitter is flashing).\n\n**Allowed speed:** `v_allow(d) = sqrt(v_c² + 2·a_plan·max(0, d − v·τ))`, where a_plan = 0.8 × B4 decel. Target v* = min over constraints minus 2 km/h.\n\n**Control:**\n- If v < v* − 4, power: the notch is chosen from the speed gap.\n- If v > v* + 1, brake with notch `ceil(req / 0.5)`, where `req = (v² − v_c²) / (2·d)` (in matched units).\n- **Final stop:** model-predictive. Simulate the train model 12 s ahead for each candidate brake notch, and choose the one whose predicted stop point\n  is closest to the target. Inside the last 2 m it eases to B1 (smooth stop). This should hit PERFECT reliably. If it doesn't, the physics or the\n  lag model is wrong; fix that, not the bot.\n\n**Timetable (needed for ON TIME and rank S):**\n- Every 2 s, pick a cruise cap by bisection: for each candidate, simulate the train model over the rest of the interval (0.1 s steps, with the\n  same constraints) and keep the cap whose predicted arrival matches the scheduled `arr` (aim for −0.5 s).\n- Coast (N) where that still arrives on time.\n- Depart the moment the departure is allowed.\n- **Min-time mode** ignores the timetable and drives as fast as the rules allow. This is \"par\" for calibration (§4.8).\n\n**Procedures:** confirm ATS within 0.5 s; make every call at the sweet spot; horn for 0.8 s at each horn board; EB on the obstruction emitter if it can't\nstop with B8; answer the S5 flash.\n\n**Adhesion:** in rain, never pick a power or brake notch whose effort exceeds the adhesion limit (B5 at most with the default 2.6 km/h/s). Plan the\nbraking with that notch, so the S2 run has no slip or slide.\n\n**Skill** (0–1, a dev slider):\n- 1 = perfect;\n- 0.6 = human-like: 0.25 s reaction delay, ±3 km/h noise, stop aim ±0.4 m, drawn from `DH.rng('auto')`.\n\n**Cheat flags** (attract): no Halo Time loss; teleport between vignettes; forced events.\n\n**Tests:**\n- Skill 1 completes every stage with every stop GOOD or better (PERFECT on ≥ 80% of stops), no game over, every scripted event fired, rank S.\n- Skill 0.6 completes every stage with no game over on Normal.\n- Its runs are deterministic: proof hash twice.\n\n### 4.20 Attract mode (`61-attract.js`)\n\n**Trigger:** 10 s with no input on **title, haloMap, freeRun setup, duelLobby, driversLog, help or about**.\n- Input means any key, gamepad button or stick past the deadzone, mouse move over 4 px, click, wheel or touch.\n- The timer is game time measured in those scenes. It is paused while a Shell screen (settings) is open, and restarts on any input.\n\n**Loop**, about 3 min, using the autopilot with cheats and the camera director:\n1. **S0 Depot, dawn:** handle close-ups in the cab; a perfect stop at Mark 1, then the Stop Cam.\n2. **S1 Hanabatake departure:** platform cam showing the doors, the melody, the buzzer and the pilot lamp; the \"Shuppatsu shinko!\" call with the glove;\n   then the horn board, the oncoming train and the chase cam onto the bridge.\n3. **S2 rain:** wipers, the SLIP lamp, ATS bell and confirm, yellow signals, the tunnel mouth, passing Todai-shita alongside the freight.\n4. **S3:** the obstruction crossing (trackside cam, then EB), the track workers waving, a chase cam on the climb.\n5. **S4:** chaseHigh with the Sky Liner alongside, the door rush (platform cam), sunset glare.\n6. **S5:** night headlights, fog, the final approach to Akari Central, the staff bow, PERFECT.\n7. **\"TODAY'S BEST DRIVERS\"** table (top 5 career totals from `E.store`, or seeded defaults with names like \"HOSHINO\"), then the logo card.\n   Then loop.\n\n**Overlay:** a \"DEMO\" badge, a small logo, and \"Press any button\" with the device glyph. On touch-only devices it reads \"Controller or keyboard\nrequired\" instead.\n\n**Rules:** any input goes back to the title (`game.go('title', null, { fade: 'wipe' })`) and resets the idle timer. Attract never writes records or\npostcards. It is never shown in hub sessions.\n\n**Tests** (headless):\n- `advance(10.2)` on the title enters attract.\n- The vignettes advance in order; after the loop's length it is back at vignette 1.\n- `tap('confirm')`, `press('KeyA')`, `feed('move')` and a stick tap each return to the title.\n- No records are written.\n- Touch-only still starts attract.\n\n### 4.21 Dev Mode (`62-dev.js`)\n\n**Reach:** the pause menu item **Dev Mode** (`menu.items`). It is not offered in hub sessions. It opens an `E.Shell` screen with tool buttons and two\nsub-screens. Everything works with controller, keyboard and mouse.\n\n**Tools:**\n- Auto-drive on/off; autopilot skill;\n- God mode (no penalties); freeze Halo Time; time scale (0.25×–4×);\n- jump: to the next station approach (600 m out), to km (a km slider plus Go), skip to the next stop (arrive PERFECT), finish the stage (perfect),\n  fail the stage (game over);\n- time of day (slider, 0–24 h); weather (Clear / Rain / Fog);\n- force the next signal's aspect (R / Y / YG / G);\n- trigger an event (ATS test, obstruction, door rush, oncoming, workers, flash);\n- camera (cab / chase / chaseHigh / platform / trackside);\n- debug overlay on/off (also the backquote key while Dev Mode is unlocked);\n- braking guide on/off;\n- unlock all stages; reset save data (asks first, in-game);\n- **sound board**: play any sfx, melody, song or Babble voice, to audition them.\n\n**Balance sliders** (live; the `Dev` group, §3.8 #3), with **Reset to defaults** (`game.settings.reset('Dev')`):\n\n| key | label | default | range |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `accelScale` | Power acceleration | 1.0 | 0.5–2.0 |\n| `brakeScale` | Service brake strength | 1.0 | 0.5–2.0 |\n| `brakeLag` | Brake response lag (s) | 0.7 | 0.0–2.0 |\n| `ebDecel` | Emergency brake (km/h/s) | 5.0 | 3.0–7.0 |\n| `adhesionRain` | Rain adhesion (km/h/s) | 2.6 | 1.0–6.0 |\n| `gradeScale` | Gradient effect | 1.0 | 0–2 |\n| `resistScale` | Running resistance | 1.0 | 0–2 |\n| `graceStart` | Starting Halo Time (s) | 25 | 5–99 |\n| `lateCost` | Halo Time lost per second late | 1.0 | 0–3 |\n| `overrunCost` | Seconds lost per metre overrun | 2.0 | 0–5 |\n| `stopWindow` | Stop window scale | 1.0 | 0.5–3 |\n| `onTimeWindow` | On-time window (±s) | 2 | 0–10 |\n| `dwell` | Minimum dwell (s) | 12 | 5–60 |\n| `callGreat` | Call sweet spot far edge (m) | 120 | 60–200 |\n| `limitTol` | Speed tolerance (km/h) | 3 | 0–10 |\n| `atsTime` | ATS confirm time (s) | 5 | 2–10 |\n| `comfortSens` | Comfort sensitivity | 1.0 | 0–3 |\n| `sway` | Camera sway | 1.0 | 0–2 |\n| `fov` | Cab field of view (°) | 75 | 60–90 |\n\n- Every system reads `DH.TUNING` each step, so changes apply at once.\n- **Debug overlay** (pixel text, top-left):\n  - performance: FPS, draw ms;\n  - position: s/km, segment and zone;\n  - train: v (km/h, m/s), a, jerk, notch, P/B effective, adhesion state;\n  - signals: the next 3 signals with their blocks; the preceding train's s;\n  - rules: the limit in force, the next stop distance, the autopilot's predicted stop point, Halo Time, score, comfort;\n  - the event queue, stage, seed.\n\n**Tests:** each slider changes behaviour live (doubling `brakeScale` shortens the B4 distance by about 45–55%); Reset restores every default; Dev isn't in the\nstandard Settings list; Dev Mode is missing from the pause menu in a session; each tool does what it says (jump puts s within 1 m; finishing a stage\nreaches results).\n\n### 4.22 Progress, Driver's Log, postcards (`55-ui-log.js`)\n\n- **`E.store`** keys: `dh:progress` (`{ unlocked, ranks, best, stats, postcards }`), `dh:tips`, `dh:board` (the attract table). In a hub session the\n  client's storage is memory-only, so nothing persists. That is intended.\n- The title's **CAREER** opens the halo map with the next unfinished stage selected.\n- **Driver's Log** (large cards, no dense tables):\n  1. **Records:** per stage, the best rank stamp, score, best stop error and perfect count.\n  2. **Postcards:** 8 cards in a 4×2 grid. Each is a rendered miniature of its scene (the rail renderer drawn into a 160×100 buffer) with a caption.\n     Selecting one shows it large with a handwritten-style thank-you note.\n  3. **Stats:** 4 big numbers: km driven, stops made, perfect stops, calls made.\n\n| # | Postcard | Condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | \"Your First Halo\" from Haro-kun | any PERFECT stop |\n| 2 | \"Right at My Door!\" from Kota | S1: Hanabatake GREAT or better |\n| 3 | \"Rain or Shine\" | S2 with no overrun and no slide |\n| 4 | \"Takoyaki Saved\" | S3: obstruction avoided |\n| 5 | \"Not One Second\" | S4: every arrival ON TIME |\n| 6 | \"The Last Halo\" from Hoshino | finish S5 |\n| 7 | \"Point, Call, Proceed\" | every call in a stage made GREAT |\n| 8 | \"Gentle Hands\" | every stop in a stage SMOOTH |\n\n**Tests:** each condition unlocks its card (driven by `debug`); progress round-trips through `E.store`; unlocks are in order; attract doesn't unlock\nanything.\n\n### 4.23 Multiplayer A: AI Hub session, **ghost** mode (`63-hub.js`)\n\n**Head tags** (after `<meta charset>` and the pasted, unmodified `session.js` script):\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-2\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"2-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"5\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Best driving score: stop on the mark, on time\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Lever W/S or L2/R2 · Call E/✕ · Horn Space/□ · ATS Q/△\">\n```\n(`hub:type` and `hub:players` are catalogue tags copied from the hub examples; `hub:players` is the local count. The goal is ≤ 60 characters and\nthe howto ≤ 80.)\n\n**Flow** (the round is the **Parallel Run**, §5.3):\n1. `AIHub.active` is true from the first line, so skip the title, attract and menus. Run the boot steps (no key press), then\n   `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [2, 4], card: true })`.\n   - If it returns `null`, play normally.\n2. Build the Parallel Run with `DH.rng` seeded from `AIHub.rng('route')`. Assign tracks by seat: id 0 → x 0, 1 → +7, 2 → −7, 3 → +14 (all four\n   tracks run clockwise for the festival race). Then call **`AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press.**\n3. **Our own card** (`hubCard` scene), drawn with the rail renderer's `chaseHigh` camera over the four trains at Shotengai:\n   - the title;\n   - \"PARALLEL RUN: four drivers, four tracks, one timetable\";\n   - the goal; this player's controls (glyphs);\n   - each player in their seat colour (names drawn with the pixel font, never put in HTML) with ready ticks;\n   - the time left from the `card` event.\n   - **Enter, ✕ or a click on READY** → `AIHub.answer('ready')`. **△, T or a click on MORE TIME** → `AIHub.answer('more')` (pressing again takes it\n     back).\n   - No other choices, and no `Math.random` on the card.\n4. On `start`: the 3 s countdown is the Shotengai departure melody. At `AIHub.time() >= 0` the starting signal clears and the round begins.\n5. **During play:**\n   - every 100 ms: `AIHub.send({ t: 'g', area: 'quad', s, v, n: notch, st: phase }, { reliable: false })` (10 msgs/s, under the 20/s limit);\n   - every 1 s: `AIHub.progress({ score, label: 'DAIGAKU · 4,350' })`;\n   - on each stop: `AIHub.send({ t: 'j', st, j: 'GREAT', e: 0.21 })` (reliable), which shows as a rival toast (\"BLUE: GREAT STOP\").\n6. **Ghost trains:** the other players' trains are drawn on their tracks at interpolated s (smoothed toward the target, extrapolated by v). Each has\n   its stripe in the player's colour and a name tag over its front within 400 m. They never affect our world: no blocks, no signals. A small\n   standings panel lists the players' scores.\n7. **Finish:** after the last stop's judgment, call `AIHub.result({ score, stats: { 'Perfect stops': n, 'On time': m }, awards: [...] })` **once**,\n   send `{ t: 'done', score }`, and show \"Waiting for the others\" with live standings.\n8. **`time-up`:** if not finished, call `AIHub.result({ score })` at once.\n9. **`leave`:** remove that ghost.\n10. **`end`:** stop input and all sound (music off, the continuous synth to 0, mute) and show the final standings. The hub then removes the game.\n\n**Session rules:**\n- The pause menu shows only Resume, Settings and Controls: no Quit, Retry or Dev Mode.\n- No `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt` or `window.open`.\n- No network except the hub.\n- No chat or emotes.\n- Options is ignored while Create is held (§3.8 #7).\n- Storage is memory-only (the client handles this).\n- `AIHub.fail(msg)` if the canvas can't be created.\n\n**Tests:**\n- A **mock AIHub** in the Node test (`test/hub-mock.mjs`) checks:\n  - connect, then ready is called with no input;\n  - play is gated until `time() >= 0`;\n  - progress comes at ≥ 1/s;\n  - the send rate is ≤ 20/s and every payload < 16 KB;\n  - `result` is called exactly once (finish or time-up);\n  - `end` silences the game and blocks input;\n  - no title or attract scene is ever entered;\n  - the Options-with-Create rule holds.\n- The **real harness** (§8.4): 2 players with auto; 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; and `scenario=host-leave`. Each must reach\n  `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`.\n\n### 4.24 Multiplayer B: local Duel, split screen (`64-duel.js`)\n\n- **Lobby:** P1 is in. \"P2: press START on a second controller, or 2 on the keyboard\" (`game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin })`). Show each half's keys\n  (§2). The race starts when both press confirm.\n- **Race:** the Parallel Run at noon; P1 on x 0, P2 on x +7. `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'auto' })`.\n  - Each viewport: the `cabMin` camera, a compact HUD (speed, notch, stop gauge, Halo Time, score).\n  - `scene.hud(r)`: the shared clock and the leader.\n  - Each player sees the other as a real train on the next track.\n  - Prompts follow each player's own device.\n- **Results:** both score cards side by side, then the winner's banner with Haro-kun cheering. Rematch / Title.\n\n**Tests:** `game.addPlayer({ keys: 'right' })`; both inputs drive their own trains; two viewports draw; two autopilots finish; the winner is the higher score;\na tie is shown as a tie.\n\n### 4.25 Settings, accessibility, difficulty\n\n**The game's own settings** (group `Game`, via `menu.settings` or Shell items):\n- Difficulty: Easy / Normal / Expert.\n- **Button symbols: PlayStation / Xbox.** The default is PlayStation; its `apply` sets the standard `glyphs` setting.\n- Lever direction: Up = Power / Up = Brake.\n- Braking guide: On / Off (default On for Easy, Off otherwise).\n- Auto calls: Off / On.\n- Subtitles: On / Off (default On).\n- Show tips: On / Off.\n- Stop Cam: On / Off.\n- Cab display: Full / Minimal.\n- Driving music: Off / On.\n- Camera sway: 0–100%.\n\n**Standard settings** (built in; don't rebuild them): volume, music, effects, reduce motion (turns off sway and flashes), screen filter (CRT/scanlines),","numLines":260,"startLine":1037,"totalLines":1945}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0131hCArUijFiY6kSmtV7Dgm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
